{
  "id": "ag-exponential",
  "chapterNumber": "5",
  "title": {
    "en": "Exponential &amp; Logarithmic Functions",
    "ar": null
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Exponential functions model growth and decay — populations, interest, radioactivity. Logarithms are their inverses, and the tool that solves for an unknown exponent.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 5.1 — Exponential function",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>An exponential function has the form \\( f(x)=a\\,b^{x} \\) with \\( b&gt;0,\\ b\\neq 1 \\). It models <strong>growth</strong> when \\( b&gt;1 \\) and <strong>decay</strong> when \\( 0&lt;b&lt;1 \\), with horizontal asymptote \\( y=0 \\) and \\( y \\)-intercept \\( a \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 5.2 — Logarithms invert exponentials",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>\\( y=\\log_b x \\iff b^{y}=x \\). The laws follow directly:</p>\\[ \\log_b(MN)=\\log_b M+\\log_b N,\\qquad \\log_b\\!\\big(M^{p}\\big)=p\\,\\log_b M. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Move the sliders to see growth flip to decay as the base crosses \\( 1 \\). Tick the box to mirror the curve over \\( y=x \\) and reveal its inverse logarithm.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "canvasId": "expCanvas",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Growth, decay &amp; inverse",
          "ar": null
        },
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "An exponential curve with optional inverse logarithm",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "expDataBtn",
          "panelId": "expDataPanel",
          "descId": "expDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "expDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "Sampled points on the exponential curve (and its inverse, when shown)",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            {
              "en": "x",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "f(x)",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "f⁻¹(x)",
              "ar": null
            }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "expA",
            "label": {
              "en": "Coefficient &nbsp; <b>a =</b>",
              "ar": null
            },
            "min": 0.5,
            "max": 3,
            "value": 1,
            "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "expAv",
            "ariaLabel": {
              "en": "Coefficient a",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "expB",
            "label": {
              "en": "Base &nbsp; <b>b =</b>",
              "ar": null
            },
            "min": 0.2,
            "max": 3,
            "value": 2,
            "step": 0.1,
            "valueDisplayId": "expBv",
            "ariaLabel": {
              "en": "Base b",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "expInv",
            "label": {
              "en": "Show inverse \\( y=\\log_b x \\) and the line \\( y=x \\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "checkbox": true
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "expType",
            "label": {
              "en": "behavior",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": "—",
            "colorClass": "indigo"
          },
          {
            "id": "expYint",
            "label": {
              "en": "y-intercept",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": "—",
            "colorClass": "amber"
          }
        ],
        "equationDisplay": {
          "id": "expEq",
          "value": "y = 1·2ˣ",
          "style": "font-size:.86rem; padding:9px 11px; background:var(--paper-2); border-radius:8px"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "title": "Exponential Growth and Decay Curves",
          "xrange": [
            -4,
            4
          ],
          "yrange": [
            -1,
            9
          ],
          "fns": [
            {
              "fn": "2^x",
              "color": "var(--fig-indigo-2)",
              "label": "y = 2^x (growth)"
            },
            {
              "fn": "0.5^x",
              "color": "var(--fig-amber)",
              "label": "y = 0.5^x (decay)"
            }
          ],
          "points": [
            {
              "x": 0,
              "y": 1,
              "label": "(0,1) y-intercept"
            },
            {
              "x": 1,
              "y": 2,
              "label": "(1,2)"
            },
            {
              "x": -1,
              "y": 2,
              "label": "(-1,2)"
            }
          ],
          "hlines": [
            {
              "y": 0,
              "color": "var(--fig-muted)",
              "dash": "4,3",
              "label": "y = 0 (asymptote)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Both exponential curves share the \\( y \\)-intercept \\( (0,1) \\) and horizontal asymptote \\( y=0 \\). When base \\( b > 1 \\) the curve grows exponentially; when \\( 0 < b < 1 \\) it decays toward zero.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Exponential form",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(x)=ab^x,\\;b&gt;0,\\,b\\neq1 \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Log definition",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log_b x=y \\Leftrightarrow b^y=x \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Log laws",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log(mn)=\\log m+\\log n;\\;\\log\\frac{m}{n}=\\log m-\\log n \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Change of base",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log_b a=\\dfrac{\\ln a}{\\ln b} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.A — Solve an exponential equation",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\( 2^{x}=32 \\).</p><p>Write \\( 32 \\) as a power of \\( 2 \\): \\( 32=2^{5} \\). Equal bases force equal exponents:</p>\\[ 2^{x}=2^{5}\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ x=5. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.B — Logarithms",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Evaluate \\( \\log_2 8 \\) and expand \\( \\log\\!\\big(xy^2\\big) \\).</p><p>Since \\( 8=2^{3} \\), \\( \\log_2 8=3 \\). By the log laws,</p>\\[ \\log\\!\\big(xy^2\\big)=\\log x+2\\log y. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠ Common traps — Non-distributive logs & asymptote boundaries",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Logs never distribute over sums:</strong> \\( \\log(A + B) \\neq \\log A + \\log B \\). The log product rule is \\( \\log(AB) = \\log A + \\log B \\). Likewise, \\( \\dfrac{\\log A}{\\log B} \\neq \\log(A - B) \\); rather, \\( \\dfrac{\\log A}{\\log B} = \\log_B A \\).</p><p><strong>Domain of logarithm is strictly positive:</strong> \\( \\log_b(x) \\) requires \\( x &gt; 0 \\). You cannot take the logarithm of zero or a negative number in real algebra. Always check for extraneous non-positive values when solving log equations.</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0\"><strong>Exponentials never equal zero:</strong> \\( b^x &gt; 0 \\) for all real \\( x \\) (when base \\( b &gt; 0 \\)). An equation like \\( 4^x = 0 \\) or \\( 5^x = -3 \\) has <em>no real solution</em>.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Solve \\( 5 \\cdot 2^{3x - 1} = 80 \\) for \\( x \\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>1. Isolate the exponential expression by dividing both sides by \\( 5 \\):</p>\\[ 2^{3x - 1} = \\frac{80}{5} = 16. \\]<p>2. Express \\( 16 \\) as a power of the base \\( 2 \\):</p>\\[ 16 = 2^4 \\implies 2^{3x - 1} = 2^4. \\]<p>3. Equate exponents and solve:</p>\\[ 3x - 1 = 4 \\implies 3x = 5 \\implies x = \\frac{5}{3}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "A culture of bacteria doubles in population every 3 hours. If the initial count is 250 bacteria, write an exponential model \\(B(t)\\) for the population after \\(t\\) hours, and find the population after 12 hours.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>Initial population \\(a = 250\\), doubling factor \\(b = 2\\), time interval = 3 hours:</p>\\[B(t) = 250 \\cdot 2^{t/3}.\\]<p>For \\(t = 12\\) hours, the number of doubling periods is \\(12/3 = 4\\):</p>\\[B(12) = 250 \\cdot 2^4 = 250 \\cdot 16 = 4000\\text{ bacteria}.\\]</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "domains": [
      "Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"
    ]
  }
}
